Q: VoiceOver announces nothing on first swipe after tooltip is closed and focus is restored (Vue.js) A: <p>This is a relatively known iOS Safari VoiceOver/WebKit issue, when a focused element is removed from the DOM, VoiceOver's virtual cursor can become de-coupled from DOM focus. Even if focus is restored programmatically, the first swipe could be silent while VoiceOver recalculates its position.</p>
<p>A simple workaround is to move the focus before removing the tooltip, or to restore focus with a short delay so voiceover can resync. I'd also recommend changing <code>role=&quot;tooltip&quot;</code> to <code>role=&quot;dialog&quot;</code> and <code>aria-modal=&quot;true&quot;</code> for interactive tool tips.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function closeTooltip() {
  showTooltip.value = false;

  setTimeout(() =&gt; {
    trigger.value?.focus();
  }, 50);
}
</code></pre>

Q: fullscreen api on mobile devices A: <pre><code>The issue is that mobile browsers have significant limitations with the Fullscreen API, especially iOS Safari which doesn't support fullscreen on custom elements.

Key limitations:

1. iOS Safari: Only allows fullscreen on &lt;video&gt; elements directly, not on container divs
2. Android: Generally better support but still has quirks
3. User gesture requirement: Fullscreen must be triggered directly from a user action

Solution for cross-platform support:

On mobile devices, request fullscreen on the video element instead of the player container. For iOS specifically, use the webkitEnterFullscreen method:

const handleFullScreen = () =&gt; {
  const video = videoRef.current;
  const player = playerRef.current;

  if (!video || !player) return;

  // Check if on iOS/mobile
  const isIOS = /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent);
  const isMobile = /Android|iPhone|iPad|iPod/i.test(navigator.userAgent);

  if (isIOS &amp;&amp; video.webkitEnterFullscreen) {
    // iOS specific fullscreen
    if (video.webkitDisplayingFullscreen) {
      video.webkitExitFullscreen();
    } else {
      video.webkitEnterFullscreen();
    }
  } else if (isMobile &amp;&amp; video.requestFullscreen) {
    // Android - request fullscreen on video element
    if (document.fullscreenElement) {
      document.exitFullscreen();
    } else {
      video.requestFullscreen();
    }
  } else {
    // Desktop - use player container
    if (!document.fullscreenElement) {
      player.requestFullscreen();
      setIsFullscreen(true);
    } else {
      document.exitFullscreen();
      setIsFullscreen(false);
    }
  }
};

Note: On iOS, when the video enters fullscreen, you'll lose custom controls as iOS uses its native player. This is a platform limitation and cannot be bypassed.
</code></pre>
